                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1098-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1098-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1098-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_hc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Hypercardioid" title="Hypercardioid" ><img id="1098-3" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ><img id="1098-4" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_wc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Wide Cardioid" title="Wide Cardioid" ></span>AKG Acoustics C 414 LTD</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The C 414 LTD was a limited-edition version of AKG&#8217;s C414, released in 2007 to celebrate the mic&#8217;s 60-year anniversary.</p><p>It is essentially the same microphone as the C 414 B-XLS; so far as we can discern, the LTD differed from the B-XLS in the following ways:</p><ul><li>The LDT is described as using &#8220;hand-selected components for optimum sonic performance and minimum tolerances.&#8221; Nonetheless, we have found no evidence that the LTD did not also share the surface-mount PCB of the B-XLS.</li><li>The LTD body has a nickel finish with brass grille. The B-XLS is black, with a silver grille.</li><li>The LTD included &#8220;luxury&#8221; versions of the standard accessories (shockmount, pop filter, case).</li></ul><p>The published frequency graphs and polar graphs of the LTD match those of the B-XLS. The stated sensitivity, SPL, and self-noise figures are identical.</p><p>The mic provides 5 polar patterns, selectable via an electronic pushbutton control on the front of the microphone body: Omni, Wide Cardioid, Cardioid, Hypercardioid, and Figure-eight. A green LED indicates the currently selected pattern.</p><p>The same LED provides a clipping indicator; if the input signal level peaks to within 2dB of overload, the green LED turns red for 0.3 seconds.</p><p>Like its predecessors, the C414 LTD used the &#8220;nylon&#8221; version of the CK-12 capsule, a large-diaphragm design with a snap-on tensioning ring and edge- rather than center termination.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/C414-LTD-rear.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/C414-LTD-rear-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="C 414 LTD, Rear View" width="200" /></a>The rear of the microphone provides two more electronic pushbutton controls. One enables a pad, which attenuates the mic&#8217;s signal by 6dB, 12dB, or 18dB (or 0dB/disabled). The second switch controls the high-pass filter:</p><ul><li>no rolloff (disabled)</li><li>-12 dB/octave @ 40Hz</li><li>-12 dB/octave @ 80Hz</li><li>-6dB/octave @ 160Hz</li></ul><p>The C414 LTD shipped with a shockmount (AKG p/n H 85 LTD), a pop screen (PF 80), a foam windscreen (W 414 LTD), an individual frequency-response graph with serial number and &#8220;production date code,&#8221; a certificate of authenticity, and a carrying case.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/C-414-LTD">AKG Acoustics C-414-LTD</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2007.</p>
